{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat CAMPAIGN.md >/dev/null && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- BUDGET ---' && cat BUDGET.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- JOURNAL ---' && cat journal.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- STATUS ---' && git status --short --branch\"","aggregated_output":"--- BUDGET ---\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 0.79 / 100.00 USD (1%)\n- Time   : 0.2 h elapsed, 17.8 h remaining (1%)\n- Phase  : normal\n--- JOURNAL ---\n# Research Journal\n\n## Iteration 1 — Starter baseline and volatility diagnosis\n\n- **Hypothesis:** The starter model's standard deviation of every adjacent observed minute return, scaled by square-root time, is miscalibrated because gaps create multi-minute returns treated as one minute and seven-day equal weighting reacts poorly to regime changes. This should hurt both 24h and 1h CRPS, especially on volatile crypto.\n- **Change:** None yet; establish official-backtester baselines at 1000 paths on two post-split regimes, then inspect standardized realized moves. The initially selected 2026-07-10 to 2026-07-12 window produced no archived field prompts: the bundled score history starts on 2026-07-26/27. Valid primary windows are therefore 2026-08-05 to 2026-08-07 and 2026-08-27 to 2026-08-29. Kill threshold for replacing the baseline: candidate must improve mean CRPS on both BTC formats in at least one window and not worsen either competition-level proxy by more than 3% across the second window.\n- **Result:** On 2026-08-05..07, BTC-24h mean CRPS was 1934.45 and BTC-1h was 426.85. On the late window, BTC-24h (2026-08-27..29) was 3433.61, rank 257/257, top-weight ratio 7.3e-13; BTC-1h (2026-08-26..28, shifted one day earlier because a later run requires unavailable 2026-08-30 prices) was 686.62, rank 256/257, top-weight ratio 4.8e-8. Market-only diagnostics showed the 7-day volatility estimate exceeded next-24h realized RMS volatility by roughly 10% in early August and 27% for BTC / 47% for XRP late in August, while a fast/mid/slow EWMA blend was near unit scale.\n- **Verdict:** Discard the starter volatility estimator.\n- **Lesson:** Small raw-CRPS disadvantages become catastrophic after prompt normalization and softmax. Regime adaptation matters more than adding complex path dynamics at this stage. The evaluator must isolate prediction directories and verify horizon coverage to avoid contaminated or out-of-snapshot runs.\n\n## Iteration 2 — Time-decayed gap-aware volatility\n\n- **Hypothesis:** A timestamp-weighted blend of 1-hour, 6-hour, and 24-hour half-life per-minute variance estimates will materially improve both formats by adapting to volatility regimes. Scaling nonconsecutive observed returns by square-root elapsed minutes should also prevent thin assets from treating multi-minute moves as one-minute shocks.\n- **Change:** Replace equal-weight seven-day standard deviation with 45%/40%/15% variance blend at half-lives 60/360/1440 minutes. Preserve the Gaussian random-walk distribution so this iteration isolates volatility estimation. Seed each prompt deterministically to reduce Monte Carlo comparison noise.\n- **Result:** BTC-24h improved from 1934.45 to 1929.29 on 2026-08-05..07. BTC-1h improved from 426.85 to 420.53 on that window and from 686.62 to 641.29 (-6.6%) on 2026-08-26..28. Late BTC-24h on the valid 2026-08-26..28 window was 2969.84 versus a field median near 2891 over the surrounding period. Short-window ranks remain depressed by incomplete moving-average history, so raw CRPS is the decision statistic until 10-day competition runs.\n- **Verdict:** Keep.\n- **Lesson:** Fast variance adaptation captures a large late-regime gain without sacrificing the quieter early window. The next bottleneck is distribution shape and asset heterogeneity, not basic volatility responsiveness.\n\n## Iteration 3 — Cross-asset calibration map\n\n- **Hypothesis:** A single pooled volatility blend is directionally sound but leaves systematic asset-specific dispersion errors, especially for high-coefficient SP500, AAPLX, XAU, GOOGLX, TSLAX, and NVDAX. Mapping official CRPS across assets will identify whether scale calibration should precede tail modeling.\n- **Change:** No simulator change. Replace the evaluator's strict loader with a partition-wise lenient loader that preserves NaNs, then measure one-day 200-path official backtests across all active assets on early- and late-August windows.\n- **Result:** Pending.\n- **Verdict:** Pending.\n- **Lesson:** Pending.\n--- STATUS ---\n## master\n?? BUDGET.md\n?? ../uv.lock\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
